## What happened

`DSA` has been **obsolete since .NET 7** (warning [`SYSLIB0028`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/fundamentals/syslib-diagnostics/syslib0028)). Apple's Security framework dropped DSA support years ago, so on macOS .NET had been using an OpenSSL-based shim. In .NET 11, Microsoft removed that fallback — `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures, the cleanest cross-platform option is **BouncyCastle**, which has its own managed DSA implementation with no OS dependency:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

Then replace the built-in DSA verification:

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

// Load your existing DSA public key (e.g. from PEM/DER)
DsaPublicKeyParameters pubKey = /* ... */;

ISigner signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA"); // or SHA256withDSA
signer.Init(forSigning: false, pubKey);
signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(signature);
```

### Other options

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **BouncyCastle** (recommended) | Fully managed, cross-platform | Extra dependency |
| P/Invoke OpenSSL directly | No new dependency if OpenSSL is already present | Fragile, requires OpenSSL on all targets |
| Re-sign with **ECDSA/RSA** | Eliminates legacy crypto entirely | Requires access to original data + new key distribution |

**Long-term**, plan to migrate away from DSA entirely — it's a deprecated algorithm regardless of runtime.
